for most of her life isabelle has been  severely disabled  she can't walk can barely talk and has  no means of caring for herself  but it's the last 18 months of this 18  year old's life that have been some of  the most difficult  the covered pandemic has just made  everything a hundred million times worse  because  we had we went from having carers in the  morning from seven  to all the children being out of school  to over one weekend having no had no  support no help nothing  but their ordeal is no surprise new  research from the leading think tank for  the north of england highlighted a  number of injustices disabled people  here are faced with it tells a story of  a region being failed on access to vital  services  we've got local governments local  authorities  that are increasingly cash strapped and  they're struggling to make really  difficult decisions about about budgets  and what services that they can provide  in terms of social care for people  and this is we know this is particularly  the case in the north of england we  really need to make sure that disabled  people are at the heart of any strategy  because otherwise it's just not going to  work the report revealed social care  funding per disabled person in the north  is over 2  700 pounds lower than england as a whole  it found disabled northerners have some  of the lowest levels of happiness and  highest levels of anxiety in the country  and during the pandemic people with  disabilities in the region were less  likely to be employed than the national  average  responding to the findings the  government told us throughout the  pandemic we have been working with the  social care sector to ensure all  recipients of care receive the support  they need we are committed to reforming  the adult social care system and will  bring forward proposals later this year  elizabeth said she hadn't noticed the  disparities up until now but seeing them  in black and white she believes it's  shocking it makes me feel  a mixture of sad and angry um that if we  lived somewhere else in the country  we would be able to get additional help  and support for for me and isabel and my  other children whereas we have to  survive almost on what we've got at the  minute  it's just  not leveling up is it  there is no doubt the pandemic in some  way has shaped all of our lives but for  isabelle her mother and many thousands  in the north of the country  life has been a little bit tougher  insane rasheed sky news manchester
 

